// marginsheet-sync. Top level is dev; staging and production are named envs.
//
// THE DEPLOYABLE WITH NO PUBLIC ROUTES. M4 section 2a ruled a third Worker so
// the Plaid token's decryption key lives somewhere no household request can
// reach. There is deliberately no "routes" and no custom domain in any
// environment: reachable only over a service binding from api, and by Cron and
// Queues later. A route added here would undo the ruling, which is why its
// absence is asserted by a test rather than left to review.
//
// It authenticates as marginsheet_sync, narrowed in migration 0023 to nine
// tables. Its credential comes from scripts/sync-db-url.mts, which refuses to
// mint one for a role wider than that.
//
// DURABLE OBJECT MIGRATIONS, DEV AND STAGING ONLY. v2 DELETES HouseholdSync.
//
// WHY IT EXISTS: a hand-deploy left durable state main cannot satisfy. The
// chain lock was deployed to dev and staging from a feature branch on 19 Aug
// 2026, which registered a HouseholdSync namespace on both. A DO NAMESPACE
// OUTLIVES THE DEPLOY THAT MADE IT, so every later deploy from main was
// refused: "New version of script does not export class 'HouseholdSync' which
// is depended on by existing Durable Objects" [code: 10064]. A redeploy cannot
// undo that; only a migration can.
//
// WHY PRODUCTION HAS NONE, AND THE ASYMMETRY IS THE POINT: production was
// never hand-deployed, so it has no such namespace, and whether Cloudflare
// treats deleting a never-created class as a no-op or an error is NOT KNOWN
// HERE. Applying it uniformly would be guessing on the one environment that
// cannot be tested first, to fix a problem it does not have. The migration is
// scoped to the environments where the namespace is known to exist.
//
// Deleting is correct: nothing ever wrote to those objects and they hold no
// household data. The branch introducing the class for real re-adds it under a
// LATER TAG per environment, never by editing this one. Tags are applied once
// and recorded, so editing a tag after it has run is the append-only rule
// wearing different clothes.
//
// WHY v2 AND NOT v1, WHICH THIS FILE GOT WRONG ONCE. The hand-deploy applied
// v1 (new_sqlite_classes) to dev and staging, so v1 is already RECORDED there.
// A delete written as v1 is skipped silently: the deploy still failed with the
// same 10064, and nothing said the migration had been ignored. The paragraph
// above already said tags are applied once, and it was written in the same
// change that reused one. PROBED, NOT REASONED: the second deploy attempt is
// what said so.
//
// CONSEQUENCE FOR THE BRANCH THAT RE-ADDS THE CLASS: dev and staging will have
// v1 and v2 recorded, so the class comes back as v3 there, while production
// has nothing recorded and takes v1. Divergent tag lists per environment are
// legal and are the honest state; making them uniform would mean claiming a
// migration ran somewhere it did not.
{
  "$schema": "node_modules/wrangler/config-schema.json",
  "name": "marginsheet-sync-dev",
  "main": "src/index.ts",
  "compatibility_date": "2026-08-01",
  "compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat"],
  // BOTH workers.dev SURFACES ARE DECLARED OFF, NEVER INHERITED.
  //
  // Production is a hostname. PREVIEW IS A WILDCARD,
  // <version-prefix>-<worker>.<subdomain>, publishing EVERY VERSION EVER
  // DEPLOYED at its own address, so the exposure was never limited to the
  // current deployment.
  //
  // preview_urls resolves to workers_dev when absent, so declaring it is
  // redundant today and that is exactly the point: the finding was never
  // "workers_dev was true", it was that AN UNSTATED SETTING RESOLVED TO
  // EXPOSED and a test read absence as safety. Closing it by a second unstated
  // default is the same mechanism pointing the other way.
  //
  // Wrangler recomputes both "for this deployment", so a toggle flipped by
  // hand in the dashboard is undone by the next deploy. This file is the only
  // durable statement.
  "workers_dev": false,
  "preview_urls": false,
  "durable_objects": {
    "bindings": [{ "name": "HOUSEHOLD_SYNC", "class_name": "HouseholdSync" }]
  },
  // THE CREATE ALONE, AND THE DELETE IS DELIBERATELY NOT LISTED.
  //
  // v1 created this class from a hand-deploy and v2 deleted it, both already
  // RECORDED on deployed dev and staging. A recorded tag is never re-applied,
  // and wrangler tolerates a recorded tag that is absent from the file with a
  // warning, so listing v2 here buys nothing.
  //
  // IT COSTS SOMETHING, WHICH IS WHY IT IS GONE: `wrangler dev --local` starts
  // with EMPTY migration state, so it tries to apply the delete against a class
  // that was never created and refuses to start with
  //
  //   Cannot apply deleted_classes migration to non-existent class HouseholdSync
  //
  // That takes the chain lock suite down, since it drives a real wrangler dev.
  // Found by running it rather than by reasoning about it: the per-environment
  // tag divergence this file previously described was correct for the three
  // deployed environments and wrong for the only one that can be tested first.
  //
  // v3 applies everywhere that has not recorded it: local (nothing recorded),
  // dev and staging (v1 and v2 recorded), production (nothing recorded, because
  // its inherited delete was refused with code 10074 and it was given an
  // explicit empty list).
  "migrations": [{ "tag": "v3", "new_sqlite_classes": ["HouseholdSync"] }],
  // PLAID_BASE_URL IS SET EXPLICITLY IN EVERY ENVIRONMENT, and production is
  // the reason. plaid-client defaults to sandbox.plaid.com when it is absent,
  // so a production Worker with production credentials and no base URL would
  // call SANDBOX and be rejected for credentials that are perfectly valid.
  // That failure names the credentials and the cause is the missing variable.
  //
  // Absent means sandbox, which is safe for dev and wrong for production, so
  // it is declared rather than defaulted. Same rule as workers_dev.
  //
  // PLAID_REDIRECT_URI is the exact URI registered in the Plaid dashboard.
  // It is a public URL rather than a credential, so it lives here where it is
  // reviewable instead of in a secret nobody can read back.
  // THE WATCHDOG'S TRIGGER. Ten minutes, derived in the scheduled handler:
  // worst-case time to sweep is the 30 minute threshold plus this interval.
  //
  // DECLARED PER ENVIRONMENT, because named environments do not inherit
  // top-level keys and this file already carries an incident about exactly
  // that: production received a delete-class migration it was deliberately
  // left out of, because omission read as scoping and was inheritance.
  "triggers": { "crons": ["*/10 * * * *"] },
  "vars": {
    "ENVIRONMENT": "dev",
    "PLAID_BASE_URL": "https://sandbox.plaid.com",
    "PLAID_REDIRECT_URI": "https://api.marginsheet.com/plaid/oauth-return",
    "PLAID_WEBHOOK_URL": "https://api.marginsheet.com/plaid/webhook",
    "SENTRY_DSN": "https://30ce4a490e0cad1da24789eee4c5471a@o4511912456617984.ingest.us.sentry.io/4511912470315008"
  },
  "env": {
    "staging": {
      "name": "marginsheet-sync-staging",
      "compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat"],
      "workers_dev": false,
      "preview_urls": false,
      "durable_objects": {
        "bindings": [{ "name": "HOUSEHOLD_SYNC", "class_name": "HouseholdSync" }]
      },
      "migrations": [{ "tag": "v3", "new_sqlite_classes": ["HouseholdSync"] }],
      "triggers": { "crons": ["*/10 * * * *"] },
      "vars": {
        "ENVIRONMENT": "staging",
        "PLAID_BASE_URL": "https://sandbox.plaid.com",
        "PLAID_REDIRECT_URI": "https://api.marginsheet.com/plaid/oauth-return",
        "PLAID_WEBHOOK_URL": "https://api.marginsheet.com/plaid/webhook",
        "SENTRY_DSN": "https://30ce4a490e0cad1da24789eee4c5471a@o4511912456617984.ingest.us.sentry.io/4511912470315008"
      }
    },
    "production": {
      "name": "marginsheet-sync",
      "compatibility_flags": ["nodejs_compat"],
      "workers_dev": false,
      "preview_urls": false,
      "durable_objects": {
        "bindings": [{ "name": "HOUSEHOLD_SYNC", "class_name": "HouseholdSync" }]
      },
      // EXPLICITLY EMPTY, BECAUSE OMITTING IT INHERITS THE TOP-LEVEL LIST.
      //
      // The delete-class migration was written for dev and staging, which have
      // an orphaned HouseholdSync namespace from a hand-deploy. Production
      // never had one, so it was left out. LEAVING IT OUT IS NOT SCOPING:
      // wrangler inherits top-level keys into named environments, so
      // production received the v2 delete anyway and the deploy failed with
      //
      //   Cannot apply delete-class migration to class 'HouseholdSync' which
      //   was not exported in the previous version of the script [code: 10074]
      //
      // which also answers the question this file declined to guess at.
      // Deleting a never-created class is an ERROR, not a no-op, so scoping was
      // the right call and omission was the wrong mechanism for it.
      //
      // SAME SHAPE AS workers_dev: an absent key does not mean "none", it means
      // "take the default", and here the default is the parent's value. An
      // empty list is a statement; silence is an inheritance.
      "migrations": [{ "tag": "v3", "new_sqlite_classes": ["HouseholdSync"] }],
      "triggers": { "crons": ["*/10 * * * *"] },
      "vars": {
        "ENVIRONMENT": "production",
        "PLAID_BASE_URL": "https://production.plaid.com",
        "PLAID_REDIRECT_URI": "https://api.marginsheet.com/plaid/oauth-return",
        "PLAID_WEBHOOK_URL": "https://api.marginsheet.com/plaid/webhook",
        "SENTRY_DSN": "https://30ce4a490e0cad1da24789eee4c5471a@o4511912456617984.ingest.us.sentry.io/4511912470315008"
      }
    }
  }
}
